`Cache_GetValue` held a **read lock** while mutating `cache->counter` and `entry->LRU`, allowing concurrent readers to race on these writes. Corrupted LRU values caused `CacheArray_FindMinLRU` to evict the wrong entry; the freed memory was subsequently reused (e.g., as string data), producing a use-after-free SIGSEGV inside `ExecutionPlan_Free` with a faulting address containing ASCII `"value"`.

## Changes

- **`src/util/cache/cache.c`** — `Cache_GetValue`: replace `pthread_rwlock_rdlock` with `pthread_rwlock_wrlock`; remove stale comment noting concurrent readers were expected here
- **`src/util/cache/cache.h`** — correct `counter` field comment from `"Atomic counter for number of reads"` (neither atomic nor read-only) to `"Monotonically increasing counter used for LRU tracking"`

```c
// Before — read lock allows simultaneous writers to counter/LRU:
int res = pthread_rwlock_rdlock(&cache->_cache_rwlock);
// note that multiple threads can be here simultaneously
cache->counter++;
entry->LRU = cache->counter;

// After — exclusive access, no race:
int res = pthread_rwlock_wrlock(&cache->_cache_rwlock);
cache->counter++;
entry->LRU = cache->counter;
```

