## Reproduction

A composite `UNIQUE` constraint wrongly rejected entities that were merely *missing* one of the constrained properties.

```
GRAPH.QUERY g1 "CREATE INDEX FOR (n:P) ON (n.a, n.b)"
GRAPH.CONSTRAINT CREATE g1 UNIQUE NODE P PROPERTIES 2 a b     # wait for OPERATIONAL

GRAPH.QUERY g1 "CREATE (:P {a: 1})"    # ok
GRAPH.QUERY g1 "CREATE (:P {a: 1})"    # (error) unique constraint violation on node of type P
```

Both nodes have an *unknown* composite key (`b` is absent), so neither can be proven to collide. The second `CREATE` must succeed. The same failure occurred mirrored (agree on `b`, both missing `a`), with an explicit `b: NULL`, on the relationship side, and — worse — when creating a constraint over existing data containing such partial entities, which went `FAILED` instead of `OPERATIONAL`.

## Root cause

`Graph::build_composite_key` (`graph/src/graph/graph.rs`) accumulated an `all_null` flag across *all* constrained properties and only returned the empty "does not participate in this constraint" key when **every** property was NULL:

```rust
let mut all_null = true;
for prop in properties {
    match get(prop) {
        Some(v) if !matches!(v, Value::Null) => { all_null = false; key.extend(...); }
        _ => { key.push(0); }   // NULL marker — still a comparable key!
    }
    key.push(b'|');
}
if all_null { Vec::new() } else { key }
```

That is the wrong reduction. A composite key is unknown as soon as **any** of its components is unknown, so this needs ANY, not ALL. With ALL, the `0` NULL marker stayed in the key and two distinct unknown keys compared *equal*, manufacturing a false collision.

The fix replaces the accumulator with a short-circuit that returns `Vec::new()` on the first NULL/absent property.

All six call sites (`graph.rs` ×2, `runtime/pending.rs` ×4) already treat an empty key as "skip this entity", so this is exactly the contract they expect and **no call-site changes were needed** — the logic stays in one place

## Problem

Two concurrent read-only `db.idx.vector.queryNodes` calls could make each other return **the same node several times in one result page**, silently displacing genuine neighbours (in the issue's six-querier measurement, a 160-row page came back with only 5 unique ids). Sequential queries and concurrent *non-vector* queries were unaffected, and the Rust engine does not reproduce it.

Full reproduction and measurements: #2848 (@ruze00 — thank you for an exemplary report).

## Root cause

`db.idx.vector.queryNodes` is served by RediSearch's vector-index path (HNSW in VectorSimilarity). Every search over an index shared **one** `VisitedNodesHandler` (the visited-set used to avoid revisiting nodes). With concurrent searches that shared state is corrupted in three ways — each can make a node be visited twice within a single search and returned twice in one page, at adjacent row positions (exactly the shape observed in the issue):

1. search entry points reassign the shared handler member, clobbering the handler an in-flight search is using;
2. `getFreshTag()` is a non-atomic `cur_tag++`, so two concurrent searches can receive the same tag;
3. a tag-wrap `reset()` (every 65536 scans) wipes the tags of in-flight scans.

## Fix

Every search now acquires a **dedicated visited-nodes handler from a mutex-guarded pool** for its whole lifetime and returns it when done:

- top-K, range search, and insertion-time layer searches acquire/release around the scan;
- batch iterators hold their handler across batches and return it on destruction;
- the tags array is passed to the scan helpers explicitly instead of through shared index state;
- `resizeIndex` resets handler storage when growing instead of refreshing tags in place;
- grow-on-demand pool, handlers reused across searches.

The change is in VectorSimilarity, delivered via submodule bumps:

- VectorSimilarity: [`fix-2848-concurrent-vector-query-dupes`](https://github.com/arnavgoel17/VectorSimilarity/tree/fix-2848-co

