Two columnar batch primitives disagree with their row-at-a-time definition about whether a slot is *unbound* (`None`, "not in scope") or *bound/in scope* (`Some(Null)`):

1. `Batch::concat` marks a slot **bound** when the only batch binding it has **no active rows**.
2. `BatchRow::to_owned_row` **drops trailing `Column::Unbound` slots**, so the owned row says `None` ("Variable not found") where the view it was built from says `Some(Null)`.

**Latent:** no Cypher query is known to trigger either one. 12 OPTIONAL MATCH / CALL {} / comprehension queries built to hit them all return correct results. Both are reproduced directly on the `pub` batch API. They are still worth fixing: both sit on shared paths (every UNION / Apply / Optional concat, and every per-row fallback that materialises a `Row`), and any operator that starts relying on the documented contract would get wrong bindings or a spurious "Variable not found".

